bin/queue.dart<pre><code>import 'dart:io';

import 'package:dox_queue/dox_queue.dart';
import 'package:my_app/config/app.dart';

/// run queued jobs: `dart run bin/queue.dart [work] [flags]`.
/// the `dox` CLI runs this file for its queue commands.
Future&lt;void&gt; main(List&lt;String&gt; args) async {
  exitCode = await QueueWorker(app: appConfig).run(args);
}
</code></pre> <p>The worker starts the providers of your app (database, cache, queue). It does not start the HTTP server.</p>

lib/app/jobs/send_welcome_email.job.dart<pre><code>import 'package:dox_core/dox_core.dart';
import 'package:dox_queue/dox_queue.dart';

@Job()
class SendWelcomeEmail extends DoxJob {
  SendWelcomeEmail({required this.userId});

  final int userId;

  /// run up to 3 times
  @override
  int get tries =&gt; 3;

  /// wait 10 seconds before the 2nd try, 60 seconds before the 3rd
  @override
  List&lt;Duration&gt; get backoff =&gt; const &lt;Duration&gt;[
    Duration(seconds: 10),
    Duration(seconds: 60),
  ];

  /// stop a try after 30 seconds
  @override
  Duration? get timeout =&gt; const Duration(seconds: 30);

  /// the queue of this job
  @override
  String get queue =&gt; 'emails';

  @override
  Future&lt;void&gt; handle() async {
    MailService mail = Dox().ioc.get&lt;MailService&gt;();
    await mail.sendWelcome(userId);
  }

  /// runs once, after the last try failed
  @override
  Future&lt;void&gt; failed(Object error, StackTrace stack) async {
    // tell someone, or clean up
  }
}
</code></pre> <p>Run <code>dart run build_runner build</code> after you add or change a job.</p> <p><code>dox make:job SendWelcomeEmail</code> makes a new job file in <code>lib/app/jobs/</code>.</p> <p>All the settings are optional:</p> Setting Default What it does <code>tries</code> the worker <code>--tries</code> (1) how many times to run the job before it fails <code>backoff</code> none (retry at once) the wait before each retry. The last entry is used for the rest <code>timeout</code> the worker <code>--timeout</code> (60 seconds) the max run time of one try <code>retryUntil</code> none retry until this time, not <code>tries</code> times. Read once, when the job is dispatched <code>maxExceptions</code> none fail after this many tries that threw an error. A timeout does not count <code>failOnTimeout</code> <code>false</code> fail at once when a try times out, with no retry <code>queue</code> <code>default</code> the queue to put the job on <code>failed()</code> does nothing runs once, after the last try failed

Callback Runs <code>before(job)</code> once, at <code>dispatch()</code>, before the jobs are pushed <code>progress(job)</code> each time a job is done. With <code>allowFailures()</code>, also when one fails <code>then(job)</code> once, when every job is done <code>catchError(job)</code> once, when the first job fails for good <code>whenComplete(job)</code> once, when every job ran, done or failed <p>A function can not be saved in the queue. So each callback is a job of a class in <code>jobRegistry</code>. Run <code>dart run build_runner build</code> after you add one. It runs once, in the process where the step happened. It has no queue and no retry, like <code>dispatchSync()</code>. An error in a callback is logged. For slow work, dispatch a new job from it. Add <code>Batchable</code> to read the batch, and <code>CatchesError</code> to read the error:</p> <pre><code>@Job()
