Annotation Does <code>@ExcludeIf('type', 'person')</code> when <code>type</code> is <code>person</code>, the field is skipped <code>@ExcludeUnless('type', 'company')</code> unless <code>type</code> is <code>company</code>, the field is skipped <ul> <li>A skipped field is not read and not checked. It gets its default, <code>null</code> or <code>Maybe.missing()</code>, as if it was not sent. The field is left out of the validated data.</li> <li>They read the raw input of the other key, and match like <code>@RequiredIf</code> (also with a list of values).</li> <li><code>@ExcludeIf</code>: a missing other key does not skip. <code>@ExcludeUnless</code>: a missing other key counts as <code>null</code>, so the field is skipped.</li> <li>They are for a nullable, <code>Maybe</code> or default field. On an always-required field the build stops.</li> <li>The fluent schemas have <code>.excludeIf()</code> and <code>.excludeUnless()</code>. There the key is left out of the output. See Skip a field.</li> </ul> <pre><code>final String type;

@ExcludeIf('type', 'person')
@MinLength(5)
final String? vatNumber; // not checked for a person
</code></pre>

Dart type JSON Query and form data <code>String</code>, <code>int</code>, <code>double</code>, <code>num</code>, <code>bool</code> the same JSON type text, converted <code>DateTime</code> an ISO 8601 string the same an <code>enum</code> the name, like <code>"admin"</code> the same <code>RequestFile</code> not allowed a file part a <code>@Dto</code> class an object bracket keys: <code>address[city]</code> <code>List&lt;T&gt;</code> an array <code>tags=a&amp;tags=b</code> or <code>tags[]=a</code> <code>Map&lt;String, T&gt;</code> an object bracket keys: <code>meta[color]</code> <p>JSON is strict: <code>"18"</code> is not an <code>int</code>. Query and form data are text, so Dox converts them. To accept text in a JSON body too, use <code>@Dto(coerce: true)</code>. Then <code>"18"</code> is read as <code>18</code>, <code>"4.5"</code> as <code>4.5</code>, and <code>"true"</code>, <code>"1"</code>, <code>"yes"</code>, <code>"on"</code> (and the opposites) as a bool. A <code>bool</code> also takes the numbers <code>1</code> and <code>0</code>. A nested DTO uses its own <code>@Dto(coerce:)</code>.</p> <p><pre><code>@Dto(coerce: true)
class UpdateStock {
  final int count; // {"count": "18"} gives 18
  final bool active; // {"active": 1} gives true

  const UpdateStock({required this.count, required this.active});
}
</code></pre> Every detail is in Types in detail.</p>

